  "Question": "In right triangle \\(ABC\\), \\(\\angle ACB=90^\\circ\\), and \\(CD\\perp AB\\) at \\(D\\). Given \\(AD=4\\) and \\(BD=9\\), find the length of \\(CD\\).",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "6",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Parent-child configuration: \\(\\triangle ACD\\sim\\triangle CBD\\), so \\(CD/BD=AD/CD\\), \\(CD^2=AD\\cdot BD=36\\), and \\(CD=6\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Apply the Pythagorean theorem three times\n\nThe three right triangles let us eliminate the unknown lengths \\(AC\\) and \\(BC\\). Let \\(h=CD>0\\). The altitude meets the hypotenuse between \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), so \\(AB=4+9=13\\). Applying the Pythagorean theorem to the two smaller triangles gives\n\\[\nAC^2=h^2+16,\\qquad BC^2=h^2+81.\n\\]\nApplying it to \\(\\triangle ABC\\) gives \\(AC^2+BC^2=13^2\\). Substituting the two expressions yields\n\\[\n(h^2+16)+(h^2+81)=169,\n\\qquad 2h^2=72.\n\\]\nThus \\(h^2=36\\). Since \\(h\\) is a positive length, \\(CD=h=6\\).",
